A.append(dict(id='henri_lefebvre',description='A philosopher and social theorist of everyday life, urban change and the social production of space. His spatial analysis asks how practices, competing forms of capital and hegemonic power actively produce space, rather than treating it as a passive setting or readable code.',identity_append='Addendum 2026-10-25: The preceding no-biography statement records the initial 1.165 research scope. Revision 1.185 promotes this same Henri Lefebvre identity to a curated individual entry; any reading-link integration must be explicitly reviewed and is not an automatic dissertation crosswalk. Distinction from Georges Lefebvre and the absence of an inferred authority QID remain.',sources=[S('excerpt','','','New OCR/rereading of printed pp. 19–22 only from the already identified Rhuthmos mirror of the Persée excerpt. Spatial description/code is insufficient; capital and hegemony actively organize space. Prior whole-excerpt reading and uncollated-monograph/translation limits remain.','supporting_page_checked','dc165_lefebvre_excerpt_1974'),S('space','','',None,existing='dc165_lefebvre_space_1974'),S('translation','','',None,existing='dc165_lefebvre_translation_1991'),S('city','BnF, Le Droit à la ville (1968– editorial-set record)','https://catalogue.bnf.fr/ark%3A/12148/cb33074710f','BnF set record dates Anthropos publication from 1968; used for the first-volume publication year, not an assertion every later part appeared then. Body unread.'),S('life','BnF authority record, Lefebvre, Henri (1901–1991), FRBNF12155287','https://catalogue.bnf.fr/ark%3A/12148/cb121552879','Authority fields checked: birth 16 June 1901, death 29 June 1991. Source list preserves conflicting 1905 birth witnesses; primary authority value retained with that limit. Distinct from Georges Lefebvre; no QID copied.','supporting_page_checked')],works=[W(1968,'Le Droit à la ville (first volume)','city'),W(1974,'La production de l’espace (French book)','space'),W(1974,'La production de l’espace (introduction excerpt, L’Homme et la société)','excerpt',support='Published excerpt, not a second claim of complete book reading.'),W(1991,'The Production of Space (translated by Donald Nicholson-Smith)','translation','translation_publication','English translation year/credit, not original French publication; translation body unread.')],edges=[E('spatialhistory','Treats social space as produced through practices and power, beyond inventories or textual decoding alone.','Own 1974 introduction excerpt, pp. 19–20 reread; conceptual resource for spatial history, with historical reconstruction and GIS kept distinct.','excerpt'),E('capitalismdebates','Examines space as an active medium in the conflicting operations of capital and class hegemony.','Own excerpt, pp. 21–22 reread: distinguishes forms of capital and refuses to make capitalism a closed, contradiction-free system. This is Lefebvre’s theoretical intervention, not a verified general law of capitalist history.','excerpt')],life=L(1901,1991,'life','day','1991-06-29','1901-06-16',limits='BnF authority’s preferred birth/death fields adopted. Its bibliography records 1905 birth variants; these are not suppressed, and no QID or namesake merger follows.')))
A.append(dict(id='raymond_williams',description='A Welsh cultural historian, critic and novelist whose historical research on culture and society informed cultural materialism. He joins ordinary ways of life to artistic creation, while criticizing both restricted cultural privilege and prescriptions that would dictate socialist art in advance.',classification_flag='Cardiff and National Library of Wales describe Williams as a social/cultural historian. Root explicitly retains the assigned theory layer by cultural-materialist resource function; overlapping historical scholarship is stated in the biography, not denied or excluded by department.',sources=[S('ordinary','Raymond Williams, Culture Is Ordinary (1958), primary essay in teaching-reader scan','https://www.people.iup.edu/sherwood/Courses/ENGL480S06/Documents/Williams_Culture_Is_Ordinary.pdf','Own essay pp. 92–96 (PDF pp. 2–6) read via OCR, distinct from preceding editor introduction. Reader credits Resources of Hope (1989), pp. 3–14, as source and labels essay 1958. Later essay pages unread; complete books unread.','supporting_page_checked'),S('catalogue','Cardiff University Special Collections, Raymond Williams’s works','https://www.cardiff.ac.uk/special-collections/explore/other-special-collections/raymond-williams-s-works','Collection biography and selected bibliographic rows read: Culture and Society original 1958, Keywords (Oxford, 1976), posthumous Politics of Modernism edited/introduced by Tony Pinkney (1989). Biography calls him social/cultural historian, critic and novelist, 1921–1988. Its 1966 Long Revolution is a listed later edition and is not adopted as original. Metadata does not verify book arguments.','supporting_page_checked')],works=[W(1958,'Culture and Society, 1780–1950','catalogue'),W(1976,'Keywords: A Vocabulary of Culture and Society','catalogue'),W(1989,'The Politics of Modernism (posthumous; edited and introduced by Tony Pinkney)','catalogue','posthumous_publication')],edges=[E('culturalstudies','Joins culture as ordinary social meanings and ways of life with artistic learning and creative work.','Own Culture Is Ordinary, pp. 92–94 read; the essay’s conjunction is preserved instead of reducing culture either to elite arts or to an undifferentiated way of life.','ordinary'),E('marx','Criticizes a prescriptive Marxist account that would dictate cultural meanings and artistic forms from a proposed system of production.','Own essay pp. 95–96 read. He retains the importance of production and restricted educational access while rejecting directive cultural prescriptions; this is criticism within his engagement with Marxism, not rejection of all materialist historical analysis.','ordinary','critique')],life=L(1921,1988,'catalogue')))
A.append(dict(id='erving_goffman',description='A sociologist of face-to-face interaction and self-presentation. His account follows how participants infer, give and unintentionally disclose information, and how performances shape a shared definition of the situation.',sources=[S('presentation','Erving Goffman, The Presentation of Self in Everyday Life: introduction excerpt, MIT-hosted scan','https://web.mit.edu/curhan/www/docs/Articles/15341_Readings/Self-presentation_Impression_Formation/Goffman_Presentation_of_Self_intro.pdf','Title page and introduction printed pp. 1–5 (PDF pp. 2–4) read via OCR. Doubleday Anchor witness; incomplete excerpt without a consulted copyright page. Remaining introduction and complete book unread; claims use read text, while edition dates use separate publisher metadata.','supporting_page_checked'),S('edition','Penguin Random House, The Presentation of Self in Everyday Life, Anchor edition','https://www.penguinrandomhouse.com/books/61106/the-presentation-of-self-in-everyday-life-by-erving-goffman/','Publisher lists 20 May 1959 for the Anchor US edition; this is not a claim that the earlier 1956 Edinburgh monograph first appeared in 1959. Later audio version dated 2020 kept separate. Metadata only.'),S('asylums','Penguin Random House, Asylums','https://www.penguinrandomhouse.com/books/61103/asylums-by-erving-goffman/','Publisher lists 18 October 1961. Publication metadata only; book body unread.'),S('stigma','Simon & Schuster, Stigma: publisher page and excerpt','https://www.simonandschuster.com/books/Stigma/Erving-Goffman/9780671622442','Publisher excerpt retains 1963 copyright; current Touchstone paperback metadata is later. Used for 1963 original-work dating, not a claim the current 1986 edition first appeared in 1963. Full book unread.'),S('life','UNLV Center for Democratic Culture, Erving Goffman Archives','https://cdclv.unlv.edu/ega/','Institutional archive’s indexed heading identifies Goffman as 1922–1982 and ASA president. Full page denied access during this check; only indexed institutional identity/life years are asserted, not a new full-biography reading.','supporting_page_checked')],works=[W(1959,'The Presentation of Self in Everyday Life (Anchor US edition)','edition','edition_publication','US edition dated 1959; the earlier 1956 Edinburgh publication is distinct and not redated.'),W(1961,'Asylums','asylums'),W(1963,'Stigma: Notes on the Management of Spoiled Identity','stigma',support='1963 original copyright retained in publisher excerpt; current later paperback date not conflated.')],edges=[E('practice','Studies situated encounters through participants’ attempts to establish and manage a definition of the situation.','Own introduction pp. 1–4 read; social inference and interactional responses are the object, not evidence that he founded practice-oriented historiography.','presentation'),E('identity','Distinguishes intentionally given expressions from impressions others infer from expressions given off.','Own introduction pp. 1–5 read. A specific resource for examining socially performed identities; not a claim of direct influence on all identity histories or an equation of tactical self-presentation with all diasporic identity formation.','presentation')],life=L(1922,1982,'life',limits='Institutional archive indexed heading supports life years only; full page is access-denied. No exact birth/death day or stronger biography verification inferred.')))
